WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.049
From the meaning behind Ken's flashy fur coat
to the purpose of a pooping dog, let's explore

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:10.096
all the subtle jokes and references tucked
away in the "Barbie" movie.

00:00:10.096 --> 00:00:14.420
One of the cool elements that director Greta
Gerwig brings to the film is calling attention

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.790
to the specific kinds of Barbies each character
represents.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:22.840
Margot Robbie's "Stereotypical Barbie" look
doesn't outshine other Barbies, like Issa

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.289
Rae's "President Barbie" or Kate McKinnon's
hilarious "Weird Barbie."

00:00:26.289 --> 00:00:27.443
"Hi, I'm weird Barbie.

00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:30.990
I am in the splits, I have a funky haircut
and I smell like basement."

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:35.770
However, what really attracts the audience's
interest are the discontinued Barbie dolls,

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:40.745
which Gerwig includes to pay homage to both
the triumphs and mistakes of Mattel's ideas.

00:00:40.745 --> 00:00:45.230
For example, Michael Cera plays Ken's best
buddy Allan, who was discontinued after Mattel

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:50.090
realized that kids were not even that interested
in playing with Ken, let alone his sidekick.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.860
"There are no multiples of Allan.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.012
He's just Allan."

00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:55.573
"Yeah, I'm confused about that."

00:00:55.573 --> 00:01:00.745
Additionally, Emerald Fennell plays the often-forgotten
Midge, who is a discontinued pregnant doll.

00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:05.630
You can see other infamous discontinued dolls
like "Video Girl Barbie," who functioned as

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:12.097
a working video camera, and "Earring Magic
Ken" who has an earring and… a big round necklace.

00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:16.350
One scene that was featured in the first trailer
and opens the film is the little girls' revolt

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.825
against baby dolls.

00:01:17.825 --> 00:01:21.600
With Helen Mirren narrating, the scene is
cinematically gripping.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.750
In it, young girls solemnly play with their
baby dolls and act as mothers, while Mirren

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:29.417
tells the audience how little girls were always
force-fed the baby doll.

00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:34.090
That is, until Barbie came on the scene, showing
little girls that the role of mother was not

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.000
the only aspiration they could have.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.680
As a result of seeing Robbie's powerful stance,
the little girls smash their baby dolls and

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.726
revolt against this motherly ideology to opt
for a Barbie.

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:48.590
The scene might seem slightly off-putting
to those who haven't seen the original source

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:54.000
material, but the "Barbie" opening is entirely
based on the opening from "2001: A Space Odyssey."

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.289
However, instead of an alien monolith appearing
before a crowd of cavemen, Robbie's beautiful

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:02.450
1950s swimsuit-clad presence dominates the
scene.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:07.220
The young women are inspired by Robbie's Barbies,
and instead of breaking skulls like the cavemen

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.810
do, the little girls smash their baby dolls
in the same intense, animalistic manner.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:16.340
A baby doll is thrown into the air before
transitioning into the "Barbie" logo, a clear

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.890
homage to Stanley Kubrick's famous match shot
in "2001: A Space Odyssey" that turns a flying

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.590
bone into a satellite.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.560
The entire scene is absurd in these circumstances,
but manages to cover a lot of exposition to

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.379
contextualize Barbie for the audience.

00:02:31.379 --> 00:02:35.470
One of the key elements to perfecting the
"Barbie" movie comes from recreating the doll's

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:36.772
iconic looks.

00:02:36.772 --> 00:02:40.960
Costume designer Jacqueline Durran wanted
to fully capture the history of the doll by

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.920
recreating some of the most memorable and
niche costumes for casual fans and superfans

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.213
alike to enjoy.

00:02:47.213 --> 00:02:53.186
The designer started with the very first look
you see Robbie's Barbie wearing: The 1959 Barbie swimsuit. 

00:02:53.186 --> 00:02:57.110
This look is featured in the "2001: A Space
Odyssey" parody opening scene, with Robbie

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:01.720
sporting a black and white one-piece swimsuit,
red lipstick, and sharp sunglasses.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.415
The recreation of this look is almost identical
to the earliest model of the Barbie doll.

00:03:06.415 --> 00:03:11.534
It makes sense to start the film off with
Barbie's origin, and Durran absolutely nails it.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:15.780
If you are anywhere on the internet, then
you've probably seen the video of Ryan Gosling

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.620
at 12 years old dancing in an episode of "Star
Search."

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.750
He stands, front and center, with a group
of dancers, and absolutely tears it up for

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.290
the whole audience to see.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:29.310
The MC Hammer pants, the facial expressions,
and everything else about the video is incredible

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.830
and totally on brand.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.459
However, nothing could prepare you for Gosling's
song and dance movies in the "Barbie" movie.

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:41.068
Gerwig truly leaned into the hype surrounding
Gosling's resurfaced video and created a musical masterpiece.

00:03:41.068 --> 00:03:45.560
Ken sings about his meaningless existence,
giving the audience more insight into Barbie's

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:46.950
male accessory.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.519
This musical number is a great callback to
Gosling's younger days and gives the movie

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:53.590
more of a quirky tone, if that's even possible.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:58.338
"No one rests until this doll is back in a
box."

00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:02.699
Barbie has many sidekicks, including siblings,
friends, and, of course, Ken.

00:04:02.699 --> 00:04:07.570
However, Barbie also has several pets, including
a golden retriever named Tanner.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:11.189
One of the quick little moments in the "Barbie"
movie that you might miss is the inclusion

00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:12.803
of Barbie's dog, Tanner.

00:04:12.803 --> 00:04:16.540
When Barbie goes to Weird Barbie's house,
she finds the dog walking through the house

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.690
and leaving poop pellets along the way.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:23.669
The dog is only present for a few seconds,
but real Barbie fans recognize it right away.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:28.419
One Barbie toy that was discontinued was Tanner,
an accompaniment for Potty Training Barbie.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:32.259
When Barbie would walk the dog, it would drop
poop pellets, and Barbie would have the tools

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:33.259
to pick them up.

00:04:33.259 --> 00:04:36.919
The problem, though, was that the pellets
were so small that they constituted a choking

00:04:36.919 --> 00:04:38.490
hazard for small children.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.789
Additionally, the toy was recalled because
the small magnet in the scooper fell off way

00:04:42.789 --> 00:04:45.933
too easily, providing yet another safety issue
for kids.

00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:51.549
This toy is one that lives in infamy, but
is certainly a hilarious addition to the "Barbie" movie.

00:04:51.549 --> 00:04:55.500
There are many Barbie universe references
throughout the film, and pretty much every

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.449
single one of them is called out by Helen
Mirren's narration.

00:04:58.449 --> 00:05:02.560
However, because of all of them, it seems
especially jarring when the reference is unrelated

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:03.359
to Barbie.

00:05:03.359 --> 00:05:07.010
Fans will notice the discussion surrounding
"Pride and Prejudice" and the feminine right

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.643
to watch it whenever they want.

00:05:08.643 --> 00:05:12.259
There's also the brief discussion about loving
Zack Snyder's "Justice League" cut and the

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:15.320
masculine urge to shove that in everyone's
face.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.979
However, there are some quirkier and more
subtle references too, like Weird Barbie offering

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:24.325
Barbie the truth or a way to ignore everything
with a high heel and a Birkenstock.

00:05:24.325 --> 00:05:27.744
This is a nod to the red pill and blue pill
decision from "The Matrix."

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:32.259
Or, in the montage sequences, there are brief
clips from "The Godfather," "Grease" and other

00:05:32.259 --> 00:05:33.850
popular pieces of media.

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:39.090
So it's great to watch Gerwig give both subtle
and blatant nods to popular culture.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:42.220
Can we get some applause for Ken's outfit
choices throughout the film?

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.160
Most of the focus in costume conversations
surrounding the film comes from Barbie's looks,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.180
and rightfully so.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.594
However, the choices for Ken's outfits are
just as incredible.

00:05:50.594 --> 00:05:52.980
"Did you bring your rollerblades?"

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.690
"I literally go nowhere without them."

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:58.360
In keeping with the theme of making numerous
pop culture references throughout the film,

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.270
there are many references to Sylvester Stallone,
and to "Rocky" as a whole.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.500
They are mostly subtle but the biggest reference
comes from Ken's outfit choice when he takes

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.070
over Barbieland with the patriarchy in tow.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:13.880
His giant white fur coat and no shirt make
for a bougie look — and a reference.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:18.349
Sylvester Stallone wore that white fur coat
in the '70s and '80s, even earning himself

00:06:18.349 --> 00:06:20.033
a GQ article about it.

00:06:20.033 --> 00:06:24.669
Gosling learns about the patriarchy, horses,
and Stallone in the real world and takes all

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:28.590
of that, including Stallone's fashion choices,
back to Barbieland with him.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:32.759
Though the coat in the film is fake fur, costume
designer Jacqueline Durran said that they

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:36.600
lined it with horse print as a homage to Ken's
obsession with horses.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:42.330
For those who are fans of Stallone, and appreciate
Ken's love of horses, the coat is a nice little easter egg.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.889
There is one particularly beautiful scene
in the movie that features Barbie telling

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:48.490
an older woman at the bus stop that she is
beautiful.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.389
The woman, in response, says she "knows it,"
giving Barbie a nice smile.

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:56.370
The scene perfectly encapsulates what the
Barbie movie is all about, and it is one of

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.356
the most heartfelt moments in the film.

00:06:58.356 --> 00:07:02.610
However, fans began speculating who the woman
is, and many were led to believe it was Barbara

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.889
Handler, daughter of Barbie inventor Ruth
Handler.

00:07:05.889 --> 00:07:09.410
Pump the brakes, though, because that woman
actually is not Barbara Handler.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:14.110
Instead, the woman in question is actually
Oscar-winning costume designer Ann Roth.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.599
The scene is still particularly meaningful,
but does not have the connection to the Handler

00:07:17.599 --> 00:07:19.666
family that many fans speculated.

00:07:19.666 --> 00:07:24.100
However, there is a big mention of Barbara
Handler and the way she inspired Ruth to create

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:28.220
the Barbie doll, so she is not totally absent
from the film.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:32.140
Though Barbara Handler was not actually featured
in "Barbie," Gerwig found a way to put her

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.920
mother Ruth Handler in the film.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.610
Gerwig's cool way of paying homage to Barbie's
inventor is by having Rhea Pearlman play Ruth

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:39.720
in the film.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.949
This is not just a glorified cameo either
— Handler and Barbie have a nice conversation

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:47.830
at the end of the film about humanity and
their relationship with one another.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.750
However, the inclusion of Handler featured
a couple of quick jokes regarding Handler's

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.940
tax evasion, which fans might not understand.

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:59.599
In the 1970s, Handler was indicted after charges
of fraud and false reporting to the Securities

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:01.840
and Exchange Commission were brought to light.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.139
She pleaded no contest and was fined $57,000.

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:09.300
In addition, she had to serve 2,500 hours
of community service.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.080
Because there were no serious consequences,
it is something people feel comfortable joking

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.720
about, which is why Gerwig used it as a quick
quip in the film.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:18.457
"Get that Barbie!"

00:08:18.457 --> 00:08:23.170
Greta Gerwig is able to explore many themes
in the film, particularly about feminism,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:27.090
coping with existence, and trying to find
your individuality in a world that demands

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:28.340
you to be perfect.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.919
However, one more subtle theme that Gerwig
tackles throughout the movie comes from her

00:08:31.919 --> 00:08:37.344
exploration of the way Barbie and Ken are
attached to one another, and who Ken is without Barbie.

00:08:37.344 --> 00:08:38.209
"I'm coming with you."

00:08:38.209 --> 00:08:39.000
"Okay."

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.562
A lot of the film revolves around Barbie's
crisis with who she is and what she was made for.

00:08:43.562 --> 00:08:48.180
Meanwhile, Ken's crisis in the film is learning
about how he can be his own person and exist

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.899
as something other than an extension of Barbie.

00:08:50.899 --> 00:08:55.190
It functions as an interesting take on the
creation myth in Genesis, and how their relationship

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:57.431
is like Adam and Eve, but flipped.

00:08:57.431 --> 00:09:00.330
Barbie was invented as a way of empowering
women.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.720
As a result, Ken was created after Barbie
to essentially function as a human accessory

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.874
to her — just as Eve was created after Adam.

00:09:07.874 --> 00:09:12.987
Furthermore, Gerwig also found a way to play
on the concept of Eve being created from Adam's rib.

00:09:12.987 --> 00:09:17.510
The director emphasizes this in reverse with
Barbie and Ken, showcasing how Ken was created

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.519
from Barbie's fame.

00:09:19.519 --> 00:09:23.600
Though most of the "Barbie" movie focus comes
from Robbie and Gosling's performances, it's

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.536
important to acknowledge how incredible the
entire cast is, particularly America Ferrera.

00:09:28.536 --> 00:09:32.980
She perfectly encapsulates what it is like
to be a woman, and the intense pressure women

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:36.250
are under to successfully make it through
even one day.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.020
Her acting is lovely and her obsession with
Barbie is relatable — but we're not here

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:40.995
to talk about that.

00:09:40.995 --> 00:09:44.630
We're here to talk about the quick little
line that references one of Ferrera's older,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.850
iconic films: "Gotta Kick It Up."

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:51.310
Ferrera's character, Gloria, has to help Barbie
get Barbieland back from the Kens.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:55.070
Meanwhile, her husband is learning Spanish
to better connect with his wife and daughter,

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:58.210
and says "Si Se Puede" to Barbie at the end
of the film.

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:01.770
Though the comment is one of encouragement,
essentially meaning "you can do it," it is

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:06.510
more of a nod to "Gotta Kick It Up" which
is alternately titled "Si Se Puede."

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:10.690
The reference could also extend to Ferrera
and Eva Longoria's digital lifestyle community

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.233
for Latin people called "She Se Puede."

